WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Imagine reviewing a proposal for a new
project called Smart Success.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.360
The research team wants to deploy an AI
tool designed to predict the

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.520
likelihood of university students dropping
out before they actually do.

4
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.860
The core of their methodology relies on a
neural network trained on five years

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.720
of historical student data. When active,
this system will continuously monitor

6
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.720
current students and automatically
generate an at-risk alert for those it

7
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:31.500
flags. Evaluating a system like this falls
to the Research Ethics Committee,

8
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:36.120
or REC. It is their job to look past the
technical achievement and scrutinize

9
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.920
how this tool interacts with human
subjects.

10
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:43.140
The first step is examining the specific
data streams feeding the model.

11
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:47.240
This proposal pulls from class attendance
and learning management system logs,

12
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.540
but it also integrates highly sensitive
information, including socioeconomic

13
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:54.820
background data and anonymized mental
health service records.

14
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.780
To address the obvious privacy concerns,
the researchers state their data

15
00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:04.600
pipeline is fully compliant with the GDPR
and all records are pseudonomized

16
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.540
before processing. Basic legal compliance,
like data pseudonomization,

17
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:14.040
is a required starting point. But
stripping identifying names from a dataset

18
00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.160
does not clear a project of its broader
sociotechnical risks.

19
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.620
We have to dig deeper. This matrix shows
six critical evaluation parameters we

20
00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:25.940
can use to stress test the proposal.

21
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:30.500
Applying this framework moves the review
from a simple privacy check to a

22
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:34.100
rigorous ethical audit. Starting with
human oversight.

23
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:38.600
We need to ask the lead researcher if
university staff will act automatically

24
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.520
on these AI alerts. We have to know
exactly what role human professionals play

25
00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.360
in interpreting and validating the
system's output.

26
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:49.640
Next, we evaluate the potential for bias.

27
00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:53.640
If the historical data contains existing
social inequalities,

28
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.640
the model could reinforce them.

29
00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.260
For example, by disproportionately
flagging students from lower-income

30
00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:04.660
backgrounds. We must see concrete proof of
how bias is detected and mitigated.

31
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:07.540
Then there is the question of
explainability.

32
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:12.020
When a student is labeled at risk, both
the student and the support staff need

33
00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:16.280
to know exactly why. If the underlying
neural network is a black box,

34
00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.740
the prediction cannot be adequately
explained.

35
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.900
Looking at privacy and governance, we
revisit the mental health data.

36
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:27.040
Even if it is pseudonomized, did the
researchers obtain explicit student

37
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:31.060
consent for the secondary use of those
specific, highly sensitive logs?

38
00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.080
Finally, we assess the broader societal
impact.

39
00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.080
Being algorithmically labeled at risk
carries a heavy psychological and

40
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:41.880
emotional weight. We need to know if this
tool will inadvertently alter how

41
00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.080
faculty perceive and treat these students.

42
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.240
Working through these questions exposes
the gap between technical optimization

43
00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.640
and ethical deployment. A highly accurate
predictive model is useless if it

44
00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.140
causes systemic harm to the student body.

45
00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:02.124
We can apply that same rigor to a high-
stakes clinical scenario.
